# Container Registry `${registry_name}`

A STACKIT container registry is ready in project `${stackit_project_id}`.

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| **Registry** | `${registry_url}` |
| **Host** | `${registry_host}` |
| **Pull path** | `${registry_host}/${registry_name}/<image>:<tag>` |

## Who can open it

%{ if length(user_assignments) == 0 ~}
This project has no meshStack users yet, so only the platform's service account can reach the
registry. Add users to the project and re-run this building block.
%{ else ~}
Every meshStack user on this project holds a STACKIT registry role, which is what makes the Harbor
project show up for them. Sign in at `${registry_host}` with your STACKIT account — the Harbor
account is created on first sign-in.

| User | Registry role |
|------|---------------|
%{ for assignment in user_assignments ~}
| `${assignment.subject}` | `${assignment.role}` |
%{ endfor ~}
%{ endif ~}
%{ if length(unknown_users) > 0 ~}

### ⚠️ Users Not Found

STACKIT does not know these users, so they did not get their registry roles:

%{ for email in unknown_users ~}
- `${email}`
%{ endfor ~}

**To resolve this**, each user must sign in to the [STACKIT Portal](https://portal.stackit.cloud)
at least once. After they have signed in, re-run this building block and they will get their roles.
%{ endif ~}
%{ if length(owner_users) > 0 ~}

### ⚠️ Project Owners

These users own the STACKIT project, so they did not get a registry role. Owners lack the Harbor
permission `container-registry.project.permission.administer` and can grant it to themselves with
the role `container-registry.artifactory.admin`.

%{ for email in owner_users ~}
- `${email}`
%{ endfor ~}
%{ endif ~}

%{ if robots_minted ~}
## Robots

A robot is linked to the platform's STACKIT service account, so this building block minted a push
robot and a pull robot for pipelines and workloads.
%{ else ~}
## One manual step, once per registry

STACKIT grants no IAM role that opens the Harbor API to an identity Harbor does not know. Access
comes from linking a robot account to a STACKIT service account, and only the portal can create
that first link.

1. Open the registry's Robot Accounts at ${registry_robot_url} and sign in with your STACKIT
   account.
2. Create a robot account named `bootstrap` with expiration **Never**.
3. Link it to the STACKIT service account. Both fields are required:

   | Field | Value |
   |-------|-------|
   | **Service account email** | `${stackit_service_account_email}` |
   | **Service account ID** | `${stackit_service_account_id != "" ? stackit_service_account_id : "see the service account in its STACKIT project"}` |

4. Grant exactly these 6 permissions. Harbor lets the robot mint only robots whose permissions it
   holds itself.

   | Resource | Permissions |
   |----------|-------------|
   | Robot Account | Create, Delete, List, Read |
   | Repository | Push, Pull |

5. Run this building block again with a manual trigger in meshPanel. It checks for the link on every
   run, so no input changes.

From then on that service account's access token authenticates as the robot, so every later robot
is created through the Harbor API without touching the portal again.
%{ endif ~}
